d a nurse manager and they wanted to  do this because what was happening in  their institution was that they would  take these vital signs down and a piece  of paper in the patient bedside and then  later when they had time they would go  to a computer and manually enter the  data and so they thought that if they  had an application on either a  smartphone or some sort of tablet that  they were able to use right when they  were at the patient bedside this would  change and improve their workflow and  would treat minor so again a nurse in a  nurse managed the ICO developed by the  IT team and the nurse team they then  released the in-house app after a couple  of rounds of feedback and optimization  but the in-house app once it was  released was actually not very popular  so there were some clinical areas where  the uptick was only 10% and after some  investigation they found out that the  app was unsuccessful because of  limitations in the mobile infrastructure  so for example when the devices were  switching between wireless access points  when nurses were basically going from  one spot to another they would lose  connection with the Wi-Fi and so they  were missing data entries because during  those hiccups or do those last  connections data was not being  transmitted into the EMR and because of  this the nursing groups would then start  to modify that workflow to get around  these hiccups so for example if there  were certain rooms where they knew that  the Wi-Fi was better than others then  they would start assigning rooms to  certain nurses so a room that had a good  Wi-Fi setup for example would be  assigned to a nurse who was very keen on  using this app whereas a room that had  more poor Wi-Fi would be assigned to  nerds who didn't necessarily want to use  this app that much whereas before they  did have these kinds of room assignments  and so they found that these workflow  modifications that they did in order to  get around these hiccups in the Wi-Fi  they actually led to a much more complex  workflow that they had previously and  then in addition to that there were also  some missing functionality in the apps  because of the technical limitations and  data security that was imposed by the  organization and then not related to  mobile infrastructure but to the  infection control piece that I talked  about in my previous slides there were  also challenges and properly  disinfecting the tablets that they were  using and so all of this led to the app  having a really poor uptake amongst the  clinical staff even though it was the  original idea was from clinical staff  and it was developed in collaboration  with clinical staff and so really I  think what this shows and how it relates  to the mobile health setting is that  there are there are many error  potentials related to limitations in  mobile infrastructure  and it's going to relate to the  particular limitation but because we  don't completely understand how to best  utilize this type of technology even in  a more structured setting it's difficult  to predict in advance what type of  errors will be made in the mobile house  setting which is even less structured  and has more challenges with mobile  infrastructure all right so I would like  to finish my talk about point-of-care  testing and error rates in mobile house  settings by just discussing a few risk  mitigation strategies that we can put in  place today in order to work on having a  really robust point-of-care testing  program in mobile house settings in the  future so I think first and foremost  it's really important to think about the  point-of-care program and to really  evaluate it for error potential and to  use the LSI guideline EP 23 a laboratory  quality control based on risk management  to help develop a quality assurance  program that addresses the key areas for  errors and puts in place quality  assurance practices for those for those  points and this we can do today I think  that we can also more thoroughly  characterize existing point-of-care  testing systems under the conditions  that are relevant to mobile health so I  think previously both manufacturers and  supportive care groups and a clinical  groups used to characterize point of  care testing systems within hospital  settings and less so in other settings  but I think now that mobile health is  growing it's really important for  manufacturers to start really validating  these in these mobile health settings  that may have extreme temperatures or  other environmental stressors and and  also for clinical groups and point of  care groups when they do their own  in-house  nation to start validating devices under  conditions that are relevant to these  settings I think that it's important to  start developing and commercially  marketing accessories for point of care  testing that are helpful in mobile  health so for example we're still  lacking a simple and cost-effective  storage container for point of care  devices that helps to protect them from  a lot of these environmental stressors  that I mentioned and so point-of-care  testing groups and clinical groups are  really left to figure out on their own  some sort of workaround for this and  finally I think going forward it will be  important to design novel point of care  testing systems with mobile house in  mind and with an understanding of the  key challenges of mobile health  environments and designing systems that  are able to perform robustly in these  types of environments and with that I  will finish my talk if you have any  questions about any of my presentation  or would like to discuss point of care  testing in mobile house please feel free  to email me at an advisory at alberta  public labs that CA thank you

