nt parts test  ordering patient and specimen  identification specimen collection  specimen evaluation before you actually  do the testing so this is for things  like specimen quality including  hemolysis like pinyin icterus  or or any other kind of specimen quality  issues the analytic phase was broken  down into calibration of the method so  for example if there is a calibration  that's due and the end user forgets to  do that correctly that would be an error  specimen and reiated interaction with  each other the actual generation of the  result and then result validation and so  result validation for example would  include running of quality controls  periodically or other performance  indicators to make sure that the result  that is being produced by your point of  care device is actually accurate  and the last phase that was developed  for this era classification system was  the post analytic phase and most of the  things in this category relates to the  report that contains the result so  formatting of the reports critical value  reporting any other important result  reporting so for example if you have  some sort of panel and there are certain  types of results there are more  important than others maybe there might  be a system in place to report those  more important results and report  recording and retrieval so for example  if you have a device that gives you a  paper printout and that printout is  manually entered into a patient chart  but a mistake is made during the entry  and so the final paper chart does not  accurately reflect the original result  that would be an error as well and so  this was this classification system was  developed in the early 2000s I think for  purposes of mobile health we can add two  other categories in here that become  important  so one would be infection control and  then the other category would have to do  with privacy security data integrity and  transmission and of course infection  control becomes particularly important  when we start using a single device on  multiple patients and this would  certainly be the case for mobile health  environments where we might have access  to one or two devices at most but we use  it on multiple patients and then of  course for the purposes of mobile health  and the whole piece about data integrity  and transmission and privacy and  security becomes very important as well  and there can be errors made in this  phase as well  so I'd like to talk a little bit about  what we currently know about errors  improve care testing so I just mentioned  the classification system that was  developed but what do we actually know  about the types of errors that occur in  point-of-care testing and then  specifically in mobile health  environments so I like to liken our  knowledge on this topic to this map here  where you can see that parts of the map  are very well explored very detailed in  terms of cities that exist and features  that exist and then the top left part of  the map is very detailed and we really  don't know very much about that part of  North America and so the things that we  do know about errors in point of care  testing come from a number of key  studies that were published by different  point of care groups who perform quality  assurance programs for their various  point of care testing programs in their  hospitals and so the key here is is that  most of these studies were done in  hospitals there are almost no studies  done in mobile health environments  mostly because of the recent order I  guess  the young nature of mobile health but  what we do know from hospital  environments is we have a good idea of  average error rates and but what I mean  by averages is that a lot of these  studies did audits of the different  point of care programs looked at error  rates but they averaged the error rates  over different types of point of care  programs so let's say they had a blood  gas analysis program they had a manual  urinalysis program they looked at the  error rates in all of those and just  averaged amounts so we have one number  instead of knowing the error rate for  your analysis programs or the error rate  for blood gas analysis programs we do  have a comprehensive list of quality  indicators and these are based on  quality considerations on different  regulatory and accreditation  requirements and then also on that error  classification system that I showed you  in the previous slide we also have tools  for data collection so we have error  reporting systems which are systems  where either the end users or a point of  care groups can log errors that have  occurred for record-keeping purposes and  also what the outcome of the error was  we also have in-person visits which  sometimes called audits these are also  also really great tools to identify  parts of the point of care testing  process that are being done incorrectly  and then nowadays we have data  management software and this is becoming  more important we're able to connect  point of care devices into point of care  middleware and then look in the  middleware to retrieve data and to  analyze that to identify possible trends  or patterns that we may want to  troubleshoot and the last piece of  information  we know which is lat in-person  assessments otherwise known as audits do  reduce error rate it's so it's important  to monitor air but it's also importance  to end-users through in-person visits it  gives us an opportunity to establish  relationships also to talk about things  that end users may not be doing  correctly and they're received much  better than if we just send an email or  just some sort of note now in terms of  what we don't know there are quite a few  things so I mentioned that most of the  studies that exist to date about errors  in front of care testing or in hospital  environments so we don't really have a  good understanding of errors in other  environments such as in mobile health we  also don't really have a good big 