e piece of the puzzle but they may not  be the best tool to use by themselves to  monitor errors and then the last piece  is about frequency of monitoring so  again we have different approaches  depending on the point of care group  that does the monitoring some groups do  it on a weekly basis some groups do it  once a year  some groups do it once a month we really  don't know what frequency is the most  optimal so it would be good to get more  information about each of these  considerations so next I would like to  move on to a definition of mobile health  since this presentation is about errors  in mobile health it's important that we  all have the same understanding of what  mobile health is so this is actually a  tricky question and I will show you here  why so these are three different  definitions from the three different  professional organizations for what they  consider mobile health so you can see  that in the first definition  the World Health Organization defines  mobile health as the use of mobile  wireless technologies for Public Health  the second definition from the World  Medical Association it's actually quite  similar so it also refers to the use of  mobile devices to provide healthcare and  you can see that the third definition  which is from the committee that I am a  part of also refers to the use of mobile  technologies to provide healthcare so  these definitions from different  professional organizations are actually  quite similar another thing that's  similar across these definitions is that  there isn't a very clear definition of  the type of health care that's provided  with the use of mobile technologies and  you know I wasn't there when these  professional organizations drafted these  definitions but I imagine that may be  part of the reason why they don't have a  concrete definition of the type of  healthcare is this because mobile house  is really in its infancy and so we don't  know how pervasive it's going to be as  it becomes more available now the  challenge is is that even though these  mobile health definitions by  professional organizations are quite  similar they're not universally accepted  and so you can see here a definition  that I found from a healthcare product  company and so they they still include  in their definition the use of mobile  technologies to provide healthcare but  in this definition the mobile  technologies and the healthcare is  limited to the patient so the healthcare  provider is completely eliminated from  this definition so really this company  refers to mobile health in the context  of consumer technologies and then if you  look at this definition  which is from a paper that talks about  providing a tree antiretroviral therapy  in rural Mozambique this definition does  not mention what the mobile technologies  at all so here they refer to mobile  health clinics and these being  four-wheel-drive trucks that are able to  reach isolated destinations and are  equipped to provide comprehensive health  service so in this definition the idea  of mobile health is not really related  to mobile technology rather it's related  to the movement of health care from a  central location out into the  centralized location that's closer to  the patient's location so for my  presentation I'm going to define mobile  health as a type of health delivery  model that incorporates three different  features so one there's a geographical  separation between an individual and one  or more members of the healthcare team  and this separation can be hundreds of  kilometres but it could also be just a  few kilometers so for example if a  patient is in a hospital but let's say  the physician that consults with the  patient's healthcare team is at another  hospital in the city so that could also  be mobile house now next the next  condition is is that the separation  between the individual and members of  the healthcare team poses a barrier to  accessing timely and appropriate care in  traditional medical institutions and the  third condition is is that mobile  technologies either reduced a barrier or  they have the potential to do so in the  future and so what this implies is is  that there may be programs right now  they don't necessarily use mobile  technologies or use them in a limited  setting but if these programs could  greatly benefit from mobile technologies  were they implemented in the future then  they  fulfilled this condition as well and so  my experience is mostly with programs  that fulfill both the first two points  and then with regards to the third point  most of the programs that I have  experience with have only limited mobile  technologies or none at all right now  but I think it's important to think  about these types of settings as well  because I think that they can really  tell us a lot and we can learn a lot  about what is necessary to have in place  once mobile technologies to become so  commonplace in healthcare that they are  part of these programs all right so next  I would like to move on to point of care  errors point of care testing errors in  these types of mobile health  environments and I'm going to talk about  four specific opportunities for errors  there are other ones as well but due to  limited time I'm going to focus on these  four so opportunities due to  environmental stresses opportunities due  to suboptimal operating conditions  opportunities due to inadequate  infection control and opportunities  because of limitations in mobile  infrastructure so let's talk about  environmental stresses first so the  issue with mobile health environments is  is that the point of care devices and  reagents in these environments may be  adversely affected by things like  extreme temperatures vibrations impact  shark humidity moisture pressure dirt  and dust and other types of  environmental stresses that are not  necessarily seen in for example a  hospital setting and so as an example I  want to show you here three different  studies that looks at the impact of  temperature variations and temperature  extremes on performance of point of care  testing reagents and so the first study  looked at the performance of cardio  and I and BMP testing in what is short  term temperature elevations and they  found that these types of elevations led  to false negative results the next study  looked at short-term exposure of glucose  test strips and mirrors to high  temperatures and humidity and they found  that these types of environmental  stresses produced measurement biases as  high as 33 milligrams per deciliter or  1.8 millimoles per liter which is quite  a significant amount depending on the  initial starting Lucas of the patient  and the last study looked at infectious  disease testing specifically dengue  rapid diagnostic tests and they stored  them for six months at tropical  temperatures similar to what they would  expect to store them in in real-life  operation and while they found that  these temperatures did not affect  performance of a test called an s-1  antigen test they did find that it  affected the IgM test performance and  that this dust show lower rear  reactivity was selected samples so what  are the take-home messages from these  studies and an important take-home  message is that you can't make  generalizations about the impact of  these types of temperature variations on  how it's going to affect your  point-of-care reagents and so it's  really actually necessary to as part of  your analytical validation to examine  the performance of your assays under the  environmental conditions that are going  to be present in the intended setting in  order to understand what kind of  performance issues you may see with your  reagents and so here the error potential  with these environmental stresses is is  that in many cases point of care groups  or end users will not thoroughly  validate the impact of such stresses on  their point  testing systems and so that and when  they do use them out in the field  there's a potential for them to be using  devices and reagents that have  questionable integrity and because of  that they produce results that are  either inaccurate or they may not  produce results at all in critical  situations and then end users are stuck  they may have to do some other  workarounds which can introduce errors  as well now in addition to environmental  stresses suboptimal  operating conditions can also be a  problem in mobile health environments  and specifically these types of  environments may not have a well-lit  area where the testing can be performed  they may not have a dry area it may be  noisy so all calm and quiet environment  is gone there may not be a stationary  and level surface to do the testing  there may not be adequate power and so  forth so for example for our ice stats  that are used in our province one of the  first pieces of instruction that we have  in the standard operating procedure is  to operate the ice that on a level  surface for the entire period of testing  and so two of our programs that use the  ice that are the Alberta stroke  ambulance which you can see on the left  that's a picture of the inside of the  ambulance and stars which is it's a  private organization nonprofit  organization that is subcontracted by  our provincial health care system so  they both use the eye stats and so I  want you to take a look at the pictures  here and stick about where you see a  level surface here that the staff on  these vehicles and aircraft can use to  put their eye stat for the period of  testing so I don't know if you agree  with me but I don't see too many areas  that are level and  we're an ice that could go so this is  clearly a problem and so the the error  potential that it introduces is that  staff are not able to follow what is  laid out in standard operating  procedures and they're not able to use  the devices and systems in a way that is  really most optimal for use and so this  again either leads to suboptimal results  because the SOPs are not being  completely followed or it may even lead  to staff trying to do work around and  then when they do these workarounds they  may be doing things that they actually  don't know if it's really you know how  what how it impacts the point-of-care  system and the results that are produced  alright next I would like to talk about  inadequate infection control and so  there are quite a few studies available  that suggest that improper disinfection  of shared point of care devices can lead  to the transmission of infection from  one patient to another and there are  particularly good manuscripts published  about this in the area of glucose  testing and the transmission of  hepatitis B from one patient to another  there's also a really good paper which  is the first one I cite on this slide  that documented that about 30% of  glucose meters in hospital environment  are contaminated by blood and so have  the potential to transmit infection from  one patient to another and so because of  this a number of different organizations  have recommended that shared point of  care devices should be cleaned and  disinfected after each use now if we use  point of care devices that are intended  for point of care testing so that's how  they're marketed like an ISTAT or a  glucose meter there are very clear  recommendations from the magnified  sure about how to disinfect and clean  these devices the problem that we run  into in mobile health environments is is  if we start using devices that are  adapted to point-of-care testing so they  weren't specifically made for  point-of-care testing but they're being  adapted for it such as a smartphone  there's usually a lack of guidance on  how these types of devices should be  disinfected in order to achieve complete  and adequate disinfection and I was  curious just what type of information is  available on this so I looked in my own  smartphone user guide and there was  nothing about any type of disinfection  or anything relating to disinfection the  other important piece here is is that if  we try to apply the types of  disinfection that we would use for  devices marketed specifically for point  of care testing it's really unclear how  long these these other types of devices  like smartphones would last so if I wipe  a smartphone down with a hydrogen  peroxide containing wipe and let it  stand wet for a couple of minutes and I  do this repeatedly just how long is that  smartphone going to last and this is  really not there at the moment and so  the error potential here is of course  that with these devices that are adapted  for point of care testing and mobile  health that we do not adequately  disinfect them and then that this can  then lead to transmission of infection  from one patient to another all right so  the last opportunity for error that I  would like to talk about today is  related to limitations and mobile  infrastructure and so mobile  infrastructure has many components and  we have basic systems and data sources  which are required to have the mobile  data available you have gateways which  are needed for secure data exchange  between your source systems and your  devices and applications  you have enterprise mobility management  which is important for being able to  manage users of the devices and the apps  and then you have the devices that the  user interfaces themselves and so  there's actually not a whole lot that  we're able to say about the limitations  of these in a mobile health environment  because we're actually still learning to  understand how to best utilize this type  of technology within more structured  settings such as hospitals and I'll talk  about an example here to illustrate my  point so this is a publication that came  out this year and it's talked about an  application that was developed at a  university medical center for the entry  of patient vital signs at the bedside  and so the idea was developed by a nurse  and a nurse manager and they wanted to  do this because what was happening in  their institution was that they would  take these vital signs down and a piece  of paper in the patient bedside and then  later when they had time they would go  to a computer and manually enter the  data and so they thought that if they  had an application on either a  smartphone or some sort of tablet that  they were able to use right when they  were at the patient bedside this would  change and improve their workflow and  would treat minor so again a nurse in a  nurse managed the ICO developed by the  IT team and the nurse team they then  released the in-house app after a couple  of rounds of feedback and optimization  but the in-house app once it was  released was actually not very popular  so there were some clinical areas where  the uptick was only 10% and after some  investigation they found out that the  app was unsuccessful because of  limitations in the mobile infrastructure  so for example when the devices were  switching between wireless access points  when nurses were basically going from  one spot to another they would lose  connection with the Wi-Fi and so they  were missing data entries because during  those hiccups or do those last  connections data was not being  transmitted into the EMR and because of  this the nursing groups would then start  to modify that workflow to get around  these hiccups so for example if there  were certain rooms where they knew that  the Wi-Fi was better than others then  they would start assigning rooms to  certain nurses so a room that had a good  Wi-Fi setup for example would be  assigned to a nurse who was very keen on  using this app whereas a room that had  more poor Wi-Fi would be assigned to  nerds who didn't necessarily want to use  this app that much whereas before they  did have these kinds of room assignments  and so they found that these workflow  modifications that they did in order to  get around these hiccups in the Wi-Fi  they actually led to a much more complex  workflow that they had previously and  then in addition to that there were also  some missing functionality in the apps  because of the technical limitations and  data security that was imposed by the  organization and then not related to  mobile infrastructure but to the  infection control piece that I talked  about in my previous slides there were  also challenges and properly  disinfecting the tablets that they were  using and so all of this led to the app  having a really poor uptake amongst the  clinical staff even though it was the  original idea was from clinical staff  and it was developed in collaboration  with clinical staff and so really I  think what this shows and how it relates  to the mobile health setting is that  there are there are many error  potentials related to limitations in  mobile infrastructure  and it's going to relate to the  particular limitation but because we  don't completely understand how to best  utilize this type of technology even in  a more structured setting it's difficult  to predict in advance what type of  errors will be made in the mobile house  setting which is even less structured  and has more challenges with mobile  infrastructure all right so I would like  to finish my talk about point-of-care  testing and error rates in mobile house  settings by just discussing a few risk  mitigation strategies that we can put in  place today in order to work on having a  really robust point-of-care testing  program in mobile house settings in the  future so I think first and foremost  it's really important to think about the  point-of-care program and to really  evaluate it for error potential and to  use the LSI guideline EP 23 a laboratory  quality control based on risk management  to help develop a quality assurance  program that addresses the key areas for  errors and puts in place quality  assurance practices for those for those  points and this we can do today I think  that we can also more thoroughly  characterize existing point-of-care  testing systems under the conditions  that are relevant to mobile health so I  think previously both manufacturers and  supportive care groups and a clinical  groups used to characterize point of  care testing systems within hospital  settings and less so in other settings  but I think now that mobile health is  growing it's really important for  manufacturers to start really validating  these in these mobile health settings  that may have extreme temperatures or  other environmental stressors and and  also for clinical groups and point of  care groups when they do their own  in-house  nation to start validating devices under  conditions that are relevant to these  settings I think that it's important to  start developing and commercially  marketing accessories for point of care  testing that are helpful in mobile  health so for example we're still  lacking a simple and cost-effective  storage container for point of care  devices that helps to protect them from  a lot of these environmental stressors  that I mentioned and so point-of-care  testing groups and clinical groups are  really left to figure out on their own  some sort of workaround for this and  finally I think going forward it will be  important to design novel point of care  testing systems with mobile house in  mind and with an understanding of the  key challenges of mobile health  environments and designing systems that  are able to perform robustly in these  types of environments and with that I  will finish my talk if you have any  questions about any of my presentation  or would like to discuss point of care  testing in mobile house please feel free  to email me at an advisory at alberta  public labs that CA thank you
 

